{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310801,"nonce":"dLMy7AjH2YiUpBIvQ6ilor4eAHI=","title":"nbody trails haxe-cpp in CI because the loop-invariant `this.bodies` load is re-issued every iteration; MIR LICM cannot fix it, TBAA can","message":"**FIXED 966215a1** (TBAA emission). nbody **0.55 -\u003e 0.42s on x86_64** (24%,\nBETTER than the 0.51 manual-hoist ceiling — the metadata unlocks more than the\nhoist alone) and 0.68 -\u003e 0.58s on aarch64. Checksums hold on all three tiers;\nsuite 183/183; other kernels unmoved.\n\nTHE DESIGN THAT WORKED, and the two dead ends inside it:\n- Tag by BYTE OFFSET, never by class. Under inheritance a subclass reuses the\n  parent's slots, so `Parent.x` and `Child.x` MUST share a tag; keying on the\n  class would split them and miscompile.\n- A FLAT tree (all slots siblings under one root) DOES NOT WORK. LLVM keeps the\n  nearest common ancestor when merging accesses, so two vectorised `Float`\n  field stores collapse to the root and alias everything again. Verified in the\n  IR: the merged `store \u003c2 x double\u003e` came out untagged. Two families — `fp`\n  and `word` — under the root fixes it.\n- Even with correct tags the load stays put while an opaque CALL is in the\n  loop. `Math.sqrt` as a runtime call pins it; inlining it lets the load hoist\n  (2 loads -\u003e 1). But inlining buys NOTHING on top of TBAA once it is in\n  (0.58 either way), so the runtime-call decision from\n  [[perf_llvm_jit_intrinsic_wrappers_and_sqrt_trap]] STANDS.\n- Untagged = may-alias-anything, which is the safe default: array elements,\n  raw pointers, and a GEP off another GEP are all deliberately left untagged.\n\nOriginal investigation follows.\n\nOPEN, x86_64 (so it IS the CI gap). Measured 2026-08-14.\n\nCI (AMD EPYC 9V74, 4 cores, run 31754113998) nbody medians:\n\n| target | ms |\n|---|---|\n| **haxe-cpp** | **750.4** |\n| rayzor-precompiled | 784.2 |\n| rayzor-cranelift | 784.4 |\n| rayzor-tiered | 816.4 |\n| rayzor-llvm | 817.9 |\n| haxe-hashlink-c | 1553.6 |\n\nSo rayzor is ~4.5% behind haxe-cpp, and LLVM sits ~4% behind Cranelift on x86\n(NUC repro: cranelift 387.8, llvm 400.5).\n\n**THE LEVER, PROVEN BY EXPERIMENT.** nbody's inner loop re-issues the\nloop-invariant `this.bodies` load plus its data-pointer load on every\niteration, because LLVM cannot prove the field stores (`a.vx` …) do not alias\nthe field holding the array. Hoisting it IN THE HAXE SOURCE (`var bs =\nthis.bodies` outside the loops) gives, on the NUC: **0.55s -\u003e 0.51s (~10%\ntotal, ~13% of execute)**. On Mac only ~3%, so DO NOT evaluate this on Mac.\nThat win alone would put rayzor ahead of haxe-cpp in CI.\n\n**MEASURED DEAD ENDS — do not repeat:**\n- SLP vectorisation is NOT the x86 analogue of the aarch64 `ldp` problem.\n  `RZT_NO_SLP=1` on the NUC: llvm 400.56 -\u003e 400.53, cranelift 387.8 -\u003e 387.3.\n  Nothing.\n- MIR **LICM cannot deliver this**. Two refinements were implemented, verified\n  correct (suite 183/183, all three mandelbrot checksums unchanged) and then\n  REVERTED for delivering zero: (a) tracking the byte offsets a loop stores\n  through constant-index GEPs, so a load of a slot the loop never writes stays\n  invariant instead of being killed by a blanket `any_store`; (b) treating\n  `haxe_math_*` as memory-clean so the `sqrt` call stops setting `any_call`.\n  NUC: 0.55 both ways. The reload is re-materialised at the LLVM level, so a\n  MIR-side hoist does not survive.\n- Loop vectorisation is not the issue either: the position-update loop already\n  vectorises (`\u003c2 x double\u003e`, `llvm.fma.v2f64`), and the O(n^2) pair loop\n  correctly does not (serial sqrt -\u003e divide -\u003e six dependent RMW stores, and\n  `a`/`b` may alias).\n\n**THE FIX IS TBAA** in the LLVM backend: tell LLVM an f64 field store cannot\nalias a pointer field load, and the hoist happens in LLVM where it sticks. The\nhook already exists and is documented for exactly this —\n`IrInstruction::GetElementPtr.struct_context: Option\u003cStructFieldRef\u003e`, whose\ncomment says \"enables … LLVM backend to add TBAA metadata\". Nothing emits it\nyet. TBAA is easy to get wrong; Haxe reads the same slot at different types\nthrough Dynamic/boxing, so a scheme keyed on the declared field, not on the\nload type, is the safer shape.\n\nRelated: the aarch64 half of this family is\n[[bugs_tiered_llvm_upgrade_reports_success_but_pointers_are_empty]] (fixed\nb41e952a) — same theme of memory-access shaping dominating this kernel.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_nbody_x86_gap_is_the_reloaded_field_load` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}